@brief
A ticket, and a vim you did not configure.

    ORD-431  Ship the refund-reason column. Migration, provider, changelog.
             And sort out whatever the shared vim config is doing, because
             the last three people to touch this repo all left the same
             mess behind.

`config.vim` in this directory is sourced into every session on this
machine. It is somebody else's file. Read it, work around it, and do not
change it — the check will fail if you do.

Four buffers, in this order.

  1. **CHANGELOG.md** — add one line to the end, as a single unwrapped
     line, and keep every bullet in the file a `-`:

         - Refunds outside the window now record a reason.

     Two different things in that config will stop you.

  2. **refund.provider.ts** — rename `refund_reason` to `reason`. Three
     occurrences, two of them on the same line.

  3. **migrations/2026_09_17_refund_reason.sql** — the three continuation
     lines want the database team's four-space indent. Only this buffer.

  4. **after/ftplugin/sql.vim** — does not exist yet. Create it with the
     three lines that make every SQL buffer behave that way from now on:

         setlocal shiftwidth=4
         setlocal tabstop=4
         setlocal expandtab

Four buffers open, so finish with `:wqa`.

@keys
| `:verbose set opt?` | the value, the file and the line |
| `:verbose nmap <key>` | the same for a key that has been taken |
| `:autocmd BufWritePre` | what is registered, and in which group |
| `:autocmd! <group>` | delete a group's autocommands |
| `:setlocal opt=…` | change this buffer and no other |
| `:set opt&` | back to vim's default |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer, write and quit everything |

@hints
Do not guess at any of it. Four commands will tell you everything:
`:autocmd BufWritePre`, `:verbose setlocal textwidth?`,
`:verbose set gdefault?` and `:set shiftwidth?`. Read `config.vim` if you
would rather see it all at once — it is only nine lines.
--
The changelog has two problems, not one. Something breaks the line you
type as you type it, and something else rewrites the bullets a moment
before the file is written. The first is an option on the buffer; the
second is an autocommand in a group.
--
The rename looks like a job for `:%s/refund_reason/reason/g`, and it is —
once the `g` means what you think it means. The option involved is
`gdefault`. `config.vim` also takes the underscore out of `iskeyword`,
which is why `ciw` is no use to you on these identifiers.
--
The SQL indent is `:setlocal shiftwidth=4` and then `2G3>>`. Use
`setlocal`, not `set`: the ftplugin file you are about to write is the
permanent version, and it would be a poor advertisement to leak four
spaces into the other three buffers on the way.

@solution
A route that measures 238 keystrokes.

**CHANGELOG.md**, already the current buffer. `:autocmd! dojo` clears the
group, which stops the bullet rewrite on write. That does not undo the
`textwidth=48` the same group already put on this buffer when it loaded,
so `:setlocal textwidth=0` as well. Then `G`, `o`, the line, `Esc`.

Worth noticing: had the changelog been the *last* file rather than the
first, clearing the group early would have prevented the `FileType`
autocommand ever running on it, and the `textwidth` step would have been
unnecessary. Autocommands only apply to what happens after they exist.

**refund.provider.ts** — `:bn`, then `:set nogdefault` and
`:%s/refund_reason/reason/g`. With `gdefault` left on, that command
replaces the first match on each line and leaves the second, and the file
looks nearly right.

**migrations/…sql** — `:bn`, `:setlocal shiftwidth=4`, `2G3>>`.

**after/ftplugin/sql.vim** — `:bn` into the empty buffer, `i`, the three
lines, `Esc`. Then `:wqa`.

Four buffers, four different mechanisms, and one habit that covered all of
them: ask vim who did it before deciding what to do about it. Every
surprise in this lesson is one `:verbose` away from a file name and a line
number, and none of them is guessable from the symptom.

The fourth file is the part that outlives the ticket. `:setlocal
shiftwidth=4` fixes this session; `after/ftplugin/sql.vim` fixes every
session anybody has on this repo, without an autocommand, an `if`, or a
line in anyone's `.vimrc`. That is the shape of a good config change, and
it is where the next chapter starts.

@start config.vim
" team defaults, inherited from the old tech lead
set hidden
set ignorecase
set gdefault
set iskeyword-=_
augroup dojo
  autocmd!
  autocmd FileType markdown setlocal textwidth=48
  autocmd BufWritePre *.md %s/^- /* /e
augroup END

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2026.09.2

- The export job streams instead of buffering.
- Customer search includes archived records.

@expected CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2026.09.2

- The export job streams instead of buffering.
- Customer search includes archived records.
- Refunds outside the window now record a reason.

@start refund.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  public async record(orderId: string, refund_reason: string) {
    return this.refundRepository.create({
      values: { orderId, refund_reason, note: refund_reason },
    });
  }
}

@expected refund.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  public async record(orderId: string, reason: string) {
    return this.refundRepository.create({
      values: { orderId, reason, note: reason },
    });
  }
}

@start migrations/2026_09_17_refund_reason.sql
ALTER TABLE refunds
ADD COLUMN reason VARCHAR(64) NULL,
ADD COLUMN recorded_at DATETIME NOT NULL,
ALGORITHM=INPLACE;

@expected migrations/2026_09_17_refund_reason.sql
ALTER TABLE refunds
    ADD COLUMN reason VARCHAR(64) NULL,
    ADD COLUMN recorded_at DATETIME NOT NULL,
    ALGORITHM=INPLACE;

@expected after/ftplugin/sql.vim
setlocal shiftwidth=4
setlocal tabstop=4
setlocal expandtab
